WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.080
[Music]
Pain

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.599
is one of the most trying experiences of
life.

00:00:11.599 --> 00:00:16.240
On the cellular level it is communicated
via special neuronal pathways. 

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.359
On the molecular level however,
pain is communicated like any other sensation-

00:00:19.359 --> 00:00:22.720
via a set of electrical and chemical signals 

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.640
facilitated by complex molecular
machinery.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.800
Along the neuronal membrane, the signal travels much like electric current along a wire.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.160
This process is mediated by membrane proteins

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.600
called voltage-gated ion
channels.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.840
Voltage-gated ion channels open or close in reaction to changes in membrane voltage

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:46.000
or membrane potential. These
proteins have special voltage sensing domains

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.480
that are coupled to ion channels.
When a neuron is at rest, its ion

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:53.440
channels are closed.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.000
A cell membrane potential is generated
by concentration gradients of positively

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.800
charged sodium and potassium ions across the membrane.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.200
When the neuron is at rest, the overall
concentrations of ions and proteins

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:13.840
create higher potential in the outside
and lower on the inside.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.040
This state is referred to as resting
potential and the membrane is said to be polarized.

00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:28.720
To transmit signal the neuron creates an
action potential which travels down the axon.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.600
The action potential is mediated by the

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.000
opening of the voltage-gated sodium
channels,

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:41.840
which allows sodium to enter the neuron.

00:01:43.439 --> 00:01:48.000
This depolarizes the membrane in the
local area.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:51.600
Neurons then repolarize the membrane in several ways stopping the action potential.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.960
First, voltage-gated sodium channels close and become inactivated.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.200
Then, to counteract the positive charge of the sodium ions,

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.960
 
potassium channels start to open

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:08.720
allowing potassium ions to exit

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.200
and lowering the voltage across the membrane.

00:02:14.319 --> 00:02:19.440
Finally, after a short refractory period
sodium channels 

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.520
open the inactivation gates and 

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.920
ion pumps restore the resting
potential in preparation for the next signal.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:35.440
In this way the signal travels along the
neuron - sodium channels open and 

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:37.760
trigger one another to generate action
potentials

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:51.840
and potassium channels then stop the
action potential in the local area.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.200
Once the action potential arrives at the
end of the neuron,

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.440
it is transferred to the next cell at a
place called a synapse.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:03.519
The synaptic region contains membrane
vesicles filled with 

00:03:03.519 --> 00:03:07.440
small molecules called neurotransmitters,

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.959
which will conduct the signal upon release into the synaptic cleft.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:18.000
This process is dependent on the
presence of calcium ions inside the cell.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.040
Because intracellular calcium is sparse
while extracellular calcium is abundant,

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:26.799
voltage-gated calcium channels are
essential in this process.

00:03:26.799 --> 00:03:31.120
The positive charges of the traveling
action potential activate these proteins

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:36.959
causing the ion channels to open and
allowing calcium inside the cell.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.159
Calcium then activates other proteins
that help the vesicle to merge with the

00:03:42.159 --> 00:03:44.480
neuronal membrane, 
creating an opening for the

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.159
neurotransmitters to enter the synaptic
cleft.

00:03:48.159 --> 00:03:51.280
There are many types of
neurotransmitters. 

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:54.720
One of them, glutamate, is shown here binding to its receptor.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:58.400
This receptor has an ion channel which
opens upon glutamate binding

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:02.319
and allows ions to enter the cell
initiating a new action potential that

00:04:02.319 --> 00:04:05.680
will travel along the axon, thus allowing the message to be passed along.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:12.959
These signals can be modulated on the
molecular level causing us to 

00:04:12.959 --> 00:04:16.480
feel less pain or no pain at all.

00:04:16.799 --> 00:04:21.040
Our bodies produce natural painkillers
known as endorphins and enkephalins and dynorphins.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:25.440
The peptide showed here is an encephalin.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.800
These natural painkillers are mimicked
by opioid drugs.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.800
One of the most widely used opioids is
morphine, which is derived from the opium poppy.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:36.800
 
Both natural painkillers and opioids

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:39.520
activate the same inhibitory receptors
called opioid receptors.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:45.360
 
Opioid receptors are part of a large

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:49.440
class of g-protein-coupled receptors or
GPCRs.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:53.840
GPCRs are nested in cellular membranes with the 
opioid binding site on the

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:57.120
outside of the cell.
Inside the cell, the receptors are

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:01.120
coupled to G proteins.
G proteins have three distinct subunits

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:05.039
alpha, beta and gamma.

00:05:05.039 --> 00:05:11.840
An inactive G protein has GDP bound to its alpha subunit.

00:05:11.919 --> 00:05:15.520
Upon opioid binding the GDP is
released, and GTP binds instead,

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:19.120
releasing the g protein from the receptor,

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:22.960
causing the alpha subunit to separate
from the beta and gamma subunits.

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:26.240
The beta-gamma subunit interacts
directly with ion channels,

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:30.960
stopping the signal. For example, the beta gamma subunit can bind to special

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:34.080
potassium channels, causing them to remain open and 

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:37.919
allow potassium to exit the cell.
This lowers the electric potential

00:05:37.919 --> 00:05:42.880
inside the cell and eventually leads to
the dissipation of the action potential.

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:46.560
The beta gamma subunit can prevent
neurotransmitter release by binding to

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.320
voltage-gated calcium channels.
This prevents the activation of the channels.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:54.080
As a result, calcium is unable to enter the cell,

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:58.720
and the release of neurotransmitters is halted.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.880
There are several types of g-alpha
subunit that couple to different GPCRs.

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:06.400
Two types of G-alpha subunits can
interact with 

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:09.840
the enzyme adenylyl cyclase.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:12.400
These are inhibitory and stimulatory g-alpha.

00:06:15.039 --> 00:06:21.680
Opioid receptors are always coupled to
inhibitory g-alpha.

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:26.240
In the presence of stimulatory g-alpha,
adenylyl cyclase uses ATP

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:31.600
to produce cyclic AMP. Cyclic AMP is
often called a second messenger

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:34.880
because it amplifies the signal
generated by the first messengers,

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:38.080
the neurotransmitters.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:45.440
Cyclic AMP can activate some ion
channels indirectly via protein kinases,

00:06:44.459 --> 00:06:53.919
Both activities augment the ion flow regulating the neuronal signal.
0:06:45.440,1193:02:47.295
and activate other ion channels directly.

00:06:55.599 --> 00:07:00.080
When opioid receptors are activated, the
G-alpha inhibitory subunit inhibits

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:04.240
adenylyl cyclase, preventing the production of cyclic AMP.

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:08.560
As a result, the signal is dampened.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:13.440
Opioid drugs are a great boon in pain
management when used for a limited time

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:17.919
and as prescribed by a physician.
However, the possibility of overdose is a danger

00:07:17.919 --> 00:07:20.560
as opioids can deactivate neuronal circuits

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:23.520
other than those responsible
for pain signaling,

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:27.680
Causing severe side effects or even
death.

00:07:29.199 --> 00:07:34.800
During an overdose, drugs like naloxone
are used to save lives.

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:41.680
Naloxone is similar in structure to
opioids and binds in a similar location

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:44.639
on the receptor, but does not activate
the G protein.

00:07:44.639 --> 00:07:59.840
As a result, it makes the receptors
unavailable to opioids.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:04.800
By studying opioid receptors, scientists
are currently trying to discover 

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:09.460
new, non-addictive compounds that can bind to these receptors 

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:13.840
in order to create safe painkillers.

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:23.589
[Music]

